      "writtenOn": "2023-04-12T12:02:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why don\u0027t we instead codify this through an enum?\n\nFor example:\n```\nenum BootargValue {\n    Any,\n    Only(\u0026\u0027static str),\n    Empty,\n}\n\nimpl BootargValue {\n    pub fn matches(\u0026self, s: \u0026str) -\u003e bool {\n        match self {\n            Self::Any \u003d\u003e true,\n            Self::Only(only) \u003d\u003e s \u003d\u003d only,\n            Self::Empty \u003d\u003e s.is_empty(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\nthen (untested but hopefully, you\u0027ll get the idea)\n```\nlet accepted \u003d [\n    (cstr!(\"crashkernel\"), BootargValue::Any, has_ramdump),\n    (cstr!(\"console\"), BootargValue::Any, has_log),\n    (cstr!(\"panic\"), BootargValue::Only(\"-1\"), true),\n]\nfor arg in BootArgsIterator::new(bootargs) {\n    let Some((name, value, cond)) \u003d accepted.iter().find(|\u0026\u0026t| t.0 \u003d\u003d arg.name) else {\n        continue\n    };\n\n    if cond \u0026\u0026 value.matches(arg.value) {\n        filtered.push(arg);\n    }\n}\n```\n?",
